A||||9||Tom88gt||AC condensation leaking in passenger side||||9||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||05-19-2001||03:54 PM||||It's getting really humid in Mississippi.  My AC is dripping lots of water on the passenger floor.  <P>I'm looking at the Haynes, page 126, figure 15.4.  I don't see an obvious drain for the heater core housing, which is where the water is leaking out of.<P>Any suggestions before I blindly tear my car apart looking for a blocked drain or whatever?<P>Thanks,<BR>Tom<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||terryk||05-19-2001||04:29 PM||||Yep, check for a blocked drain. The blower motor will rust out if you don't do it soon.<P>The drain tube is on the bottom left side of the blower housing opposite the fan.<BR>TK||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tom88gt||05-24-2001||09:39 AM||||Finally found the drain hose.  Pulled it off and it is completely dry inside.  Ran the AC a bunch with the hose off.  No water from either hole.  Left the hose on and ran the AC some more, pulled hose and still no water to be found.  But the water is coming from somewhere.  <P>I know it's not the heater core casue it only drips when the AC is running so it has to be condensation.  I looked under the car everywhere for a drain hose but could not find anything.||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||05-24-2001||10:40 AM||||If it's not the drain line, then it's inside the heater/ac box. you'll have to pull it apart.<P>The bottom of the heater core cover is suposed to have a gasket. If that's failed it could be part of your problem. You may also have junk in the AC box blocking the drains. Could be a combination of both....||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||05-24-2001||12:37 PM||||Since you're on the subject, where is that drain line located? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  My gasket is bad and I might as well check the drain for clogs when I get to fixing the seal for this summer.  Passengers tend to complain about getting dripped on with unidentified fluid. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Max The Chainsaw||05-24-2001||01:21 PM||||Check the Haynes manual page 130 picture 22.11, items 38-39-& 40. That's the drain.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||c m west||05-24-2001||03:47 PM||||leaking heater core - replace it - period||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||06-09-2001||10:59 AM||||Finally got back to my drip problem.  After much searching, I found the drain hose outlet under the car.  It was hidden behind the 2 heater core hoses.  I stuck a flexible probe into the hose to try to clear out any blockage.  Nothing seemed to come out but when I ran the AC, water started dripping under the car is the same location.  I'll have to wait for a really humid day before I'll know if the problem is solved...I really don't want to have to disassemble the under dash unit to unclog the drain.||||reg||7
Z||000008||Toddster||06-10-2001||01:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>I know it's not the heater core casue it only drips when the AC is running so it has to be condensation.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Famous last words! I know its not...<P>If it is leaking inside the car it sounds like the heater core. There is an easy test. turn on the defroster and see if a mist starts coming out of the defroster vents on the dash. If so, its a bad core.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tom88gt||06-11-2001||09:38 AM||||If its the heater core, wouldn't it leak all the time and wouldn't it be green with antifreeze?<P>It ONLY drips/leaks when the A/C is used.<P>I can't see how it could possibly be the heater core, unless I misunderstand how the core is plumbed. ||||reg||5
